Prim’s Algorithm and Kruskal’s Algorithm are both used to find the Minimum Spanning Tree (MST) of a graph.
Dijkstra’s Algorithm and Bellman-Ford Algorithm are both used to solve the Shortest Path problem.

Prim's and Kruskal's for Minimum Spanning Tree (MST):
-----Prim's Algorithm:
MST for a connected, undirected graph.
Efficient for dense graphs.
-----Kruskal's Algorithm:
MST for a connected, undirected graph.
Efficient for sparse graphs.

Dijkstra's and Bellman-Ford for Shortest Path:
-----Dijkstra’s Algorithm:
Solves single-source shortest path for graphs with non-negative edge weights.
More efficient than Bellman-Ford but can’t handle negative weights.
Use Dijkstra’s when edge weights are non-negative, and you need an efficient solution.
-----Bellman-Ford Algorithm:
Solves single-source shortest path for graphs, even with negative edge weights.
Also detects negative-weight cycles in a graph. Use Bellman-Ford when the graph might have negative weights or you need to detect negative cycles.

Difference between prims and Kruskal:
first one for Prim's second for Kruskal's
---Graph Type	
Works best with dense graphs (many edges)	
Works best with sparse graphs (fewer edges)
---How It Works	
Starts at any vertex and expands outward by choosing the smallest edge that connects the growing tree to a new vertex.	
Starts by sorting all edges and adding edges in increasing order of weight, ensuring no cycles.
---Data Structure Used	
Priority queue (min-heap) to track the smallest edge.	
Disjoint-set (Union-Find) to detect cycles.
---Edge Selection	
Selects edges based on connectivity from already included vertices.	
Selects edges globally based on the smallest available edge, regardless of connectivity.
---Cycle Detection	
No explicit cycle detection needed since it grows from a single vertex.	
Requires explicit cycle detection (handled by Union-Find).
---Initial Step	
Start from any vertex (grows a tree from a vertex).	
Start with an empty edge set (builds from individual edges).
---Implementation Complexity	
Slightly more complex due to priority queue and vertex tracking.	
Relatively simple; involves sorting edges and using Union-Find.
---Time Complexity	
O(E log V) (priority queue-based implementation).	
O(E log E) or O(E log V) (due to edge sorting).

When to use what:
-----Use Prim’s Algorithm:
When the graph is dense.
When working with adjacency matrices.
When you need to grow the MST from a specific starting vertex.
-----Use Kruskal’s Algorithm:
When the graph is sparse.
When the graph is given as an edge list.
When sorting edges and cycle detection is more intuitive to implement.



//Prims Algorithm
#include<iostream> //for input and output
#include<vector>
#include<queue> //for priority queue
#include<math.h> //for numeric_limits
using namespace std;
struct compare{ //the way priority queue will be organized
  bool operator() (pair<int, pair<int,int>> const & a, pair<int, pair<int,int>> const & b)
      { return a.first>b.first; } //we are comparing two pairs (pair<int (weight), pair<int,int>> the vertices of the weight)), here be careful on the fact that even though it is a min heap it is written as ">" symbol and not as "<", it is just a weird way how priority queue works
};
int main(){
    int graph[100][100]; //the 2D array that stores the adjacency matrix
    int n_vertex,n_edge;
    cout<<"Enter the number of vertices:";
    cin>>n_vertex;
    cout<<"Enter the number of edges:";
    cin>>n_edge;
    for(int i=0;i<=n_vertex;i++){
        for(int j=0;j<=n_vertex;j++){
            graph[i][j]=0; //initialize the entire adjacency matrix to 0, there are no edges between any pair of the vertices and the weight is 0
        }
    }
    cout<<"Enter the weigth and vertex values:";
    for(int i=0;i<n_edge;i++){
        int u,v,w;
        cin>>u>>v>>w; //here u and v are vertices and w is the weight
        graph[u][v]=w; //as undirected graph so weight is the same both ways  
        graph[v][u]=w;
    }
    for(int i=0;i<n_vertex;i++){
        for(int j=0;j<n_vertex;j++){
            cout<<graph[i][j]<<" ";
            cout<<'\n';
        }
    }
    int visited[n_vertex+10]; //this array tracks whether a vertex is added to the MST, if yes(1) then the vertex is visited
    int parent[n_vertex+10]; //this arrays stores the parent of each vertex in MST
    int distance[n_vertex+10]; //this array stores the minimum distance from the MST to each vertex
    int source;
    priority_queue<pair<int,pair<int,int>>,vector<pair<int,pair<int,int>>>,compare> queue_edges; //priority_queue <Type, Container, Compare> name ; here type is pair<weight,pair<the two vertices>, container vector, compare is customized.
    for(int j=0;j<n_vertex;j++){
        visited[j]=0; //all the vertices are initially unvisited
        parent[j]=-1; //no vertex has a parent yet
        distance[j]=numeric_limits<int>::max(); //the distace of all the vertices except source is set to infinity
    }
    cout<<"Enter source:";
    cin>>source;
    parent[source]=0; //parent of the source vertex is 0
    distance[source]=0; //parent to parent distance is 0
    visited[source]=1; //marks the source vertex as visited by 1
    for(int i=0;i<n_vertex;i++){
        if(graph[source][i]!=0){ //source to i is not 0 (connceted)
            queue_edges.push(make_pair(graph[source][i],make_pair(source,i))); //pushing an edge into a priority queue,this edge is represented as a pair, the first element is the weight of the edge, and the second element is also a pair of the two vertices of the weight.
        }
    }
    while(!queue_edges.empty()){ //this loop runs until the priority queue is ended
        int u,v,w;
        w=queue_edges.top().first; //get the smallest weight based in the edges that have been added to the queue so far, at first from source to the visited vertices it will grow
        u=queue_edges.top().second.first; //taken weights vertices
        v=queue_edges.top().second.second;
        queue_edges.pop();
        if((visited[v]==0) && (distance[v]>graph[u][v])){ //if vertex v has not been visited and weight of the edge (graph[u][v]) is smaller than the current vertex (distance[v]), the edge is added to the MST
            parent[v]=u; //sets u as the parent of v
            distance[v]=graph[u][v]; //update the distance
            for(int i=0;i<n_vertex;i++){
                if(graph[v][i]!=0){ //at first we searched for the source and its connceted vertices, from that connceted ones we will make new queue
                    queue_edges.push(make_pair(graph[v][i],make_pair(v,i)));
                }
                visited[v]=1; //the vertex has been visited and so does its adjacent lists
            }
        }
    }
    for(int j=0;j<n_vertex;j++){ //final MST
        cout<<parent[j]<<" "<<j<<" "<<graph[j][parent[j]]<<'\n';
        //parent vertex of j, the current vertex j, the weight of the edge connecting j to its parent (j and parentof j)
    }
    return 0;
}

//Kruskal's algorithm
#include<iostream>
#include<vector>
#include<algorithm>
using namespace std;
int n_vertex,n_edge,p[100],rankk[100];
void make_set(){
    for(int i=0;i<n_vertex;i++){ //all the vertices
        p[i]=i;//initially each vertex is their own parents
        rankk[i]=0; //and their height is 0
    }
}
int find_set(int x){
    if(p[x]==x){ //if the vertice and its parent is the same then return as it is the root parent
        return x;
    }
    else{
        return p[x]=find_set(p[x]); //path compression
        //it flattens the tree by making each node point directly to the root during the Find operation
    }
}
int union_sets(int s1,int s2){
    int r1,r2;
    r1=find_set(s1); //parent of s1 is r1
    r2=find_set(s2); //parent of s2 is r2
    if(r1==r2){ //if r1 and r2 is the same then that would mean they belong to the same set, already are in union, no need to do that again, return 0
        return 0;
    }
    if(rankk[r1]>rankk[r2]){ //if r1 has gretaer height then make r1 , r2's parent
        p[r2]=r1;
    }
    else{
        p[r1]=r2; //if r2 is gretaer then make r2 the parent
        if(rankk[r1]==rankk[r2]) rankk[r2]++; //if both of their height is same then the merged ones rank will be one level deeper
    }
    return 1;
}
int main(){
    vector<pair<int, pair<int,int>>> graph1; //pair<weight, pair<two vertices of the weight>>>
    int i,u,v,w;
    cout<<"Enter the number of vertices:";
    cin>>n_vertex;
    cout<<"Enter the number of edges:";
    cin>>n_edge;
    cout<<"Enter the graph:";
    for(int i=0;i<n_edge;i++){
        cin>>u>>v>>w;
        graph1.push_back(make_pair(w,make_pair(u,v)));
    }
    sort(graph1.begin(),graph1.end()); //sorting the graph in ascending order
    cout<<"Sorted graph according in ascending order to the edges:\n";
    for(int i=0;i<n_edge;i++){ //printing the graph to lower to greater of the weight
        cout<<"<"<<graph1[i].second.first<<","<<graph1[i].second.second<<">---"<<graph1[i].first<<'\n';
    }
    cout<<'\n';
    make_set(); 
    cout<<"Minimum spanning tree: \n";
    for(int i=0; i<n_edge; i++){
        u=graph1[i].second.first; //from the shortest weight's edge u and v to eventual ones
        v=graph1[i].second.second;
        if(union_sets(u,v)!=0){ //if the union does not form a cycle, then add it 
            cout<<"<"<<u<<","<<v<<">---"<<graph1[i].first<<'\n';
        }
    }
    return 0;
}
